    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A surface cleaner is supposed to end the stripes a wand leaves, so it is frustrating when the dry slab shows them anyway. The good news is that the pattern itself tells you what went wrong. Straight bands mean overlap. Swirls and arcs mean speed or spin. A uniform haze means the rinse. Work through the causes below in order, and you will usually find the problem within the first three.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="read" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Read the Pattern Before You Change Anything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Let the slab dry completely before diagnosing. Wet concrete hides almost everything; streaks appear as the surface dries and the cleaner and dirtier areas reflect light differently. Look at the slab from a low angle in daylight, ideally from both ends. What looks like one problem from the house can look like another from the street.
          </p>

          <p>
            Straight, parallel bands running the length of your lanes almost always point to overlap. Each band marks the outer edge of a pass, where the spinning bar spent the least time. If the bands are the same width as your deck, you were stepping over a full deck width each lane instead of overlapping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repeating arcs or swirls, sometimes called zebra stripes or rainbows, point to the bar not sweeping each spot enough times. That is either moving too fast or a bar spinning too slowly, often because the washer cannot supply enough flow for the deck. A dull, even haze across the whole slab is usually dried slurry from a skipped or poor rinse.
          </p>

          <p>
            One-sided marks, such as a crescent that always appears on the same side of each pass, suggest the deck is tilted, a caster is worn or one nozzle is blocked. Write down what you see before changing anything. Fixing one variable at a time is the only way to know which change helped.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pattern</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Most Likely Cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Check First</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Straight bands, one per lane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not enough overlap</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lane spacing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Arcs or swirls</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moving too fast or bar spinning slowly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Travel speed, then GPM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Crescent on one side</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tilted deck, worn caster, blocked nozzle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deck level and nozzles</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Even haze</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dried slurry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rinse step</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Patchy clean and dirty areas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Uneven soiling, no pre-treat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stains and organic growth</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="overlap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Cause 1: Not Enough Overlap Between Lanes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A spinning bar does not clean evenly across its full diameter. The jets pass over the centre of the deck's path more often than the outer edge, so the edge of every pass is lighter-cleaned than the middle. When lanes sit side by side with no overlap, those lighter edges line up into visible bands.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is to overlap each lane by roughly a third to a half of the deck's diameter. On a 15-inch deck, step over five to seven inches; on a 20-inch deck, seven to ten. It feels slow, and it is, but it puts the strongest part of each pass over the weakest part of the last. Our <a href="/how-to-use-a-pressure-washer-surface-cleaner/">surface cleaner technique guide</a> shows lane patterns that make overlap easy to judge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep lanes straight. Wandering paths make overlap inconsistent, so some areas get double coverage and others none. Use an expansion joint, the edge of the slab or a string line as a guide for the first lane, then keep each following lane parallel to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do the perimeter first and fill the middle afterwards. Ending with a single edging pass along walls and curbs leaves a band that looks different from the field, because it was cleaned on its own rather than blended with overlapping passes.
          </p>

          <p>
            A cheap way to check your overlap is to look at the wet slab immediately after a pass. The cleaned lane is usually a shade lighter than the uncleaned concrete beside it. Line the centre of the deck up with the outer edge of that lighter lane on the next pass, and you will land close to half-deck overlap without measuring anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Cause 2: Moving the Deck Too Fast
          </h2>

          <p>
            Speed is the most common cause of swirl marks. Each jet traces an arc as the bar spins, and the deck's forward motion stretches those arcs into a spiral. Move slowly and the arcs overlap densely, cleaning evenly. Move quickly and gaps open between them, which show up as a repeating rainbow pattern once the concrete dries.
          </p>

          <p>
            A common guideline is about one foot of travel every one to two seconds, slower on textured, stamped or heavily soiled concrete. If you have been walking at a normal pace, halve it and test a small area. The difference on the dry slab is usually obvious.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the surface as you go. If you can see a distinct clean arc and a dirty arc next to each other immediately behind the deck, you are outrunning the bar. The wet surface behind a properly paced deck should look uniformly lighter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the pace consistent. Speeding up toward the end of a lane, or when you get tired, leaves lighter patches in exactly those areas. Shorter lanes and breaks help more than trying to finish faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            Texture changes the right speed. Broom-finished and exposed-aggregate concrete has more surface area and deeper pockets for dirt, so the jets need more passes over each spot. Stamped concrete is the most demanding of all. Smooth, steel-troweled garage floors are the most forgiving and tolerate a slightly quicker pace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cause 3: Not Enough Flow to Spin the Bar
          </h2>

          <p>
            The bar is spun by the water leaving its nozzles, so its speed depends on the flow your washer supplies. A deck built for a 4 GPM gas washer will turn slowly on a 2 GPM electric machine, and a slow bar leaves swirls even at a careful walking pace. If you have fixed overlap and speed and still see arcs, flow is the next suspect.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check your washer's rated GPM against the deck's recommended range. As a rough guide, 12 to 14 inch decks suit about 1.5 to 2.0 GPM, 15 to 16 inch decks about 2.3 to 3.0 GPM, and 18 to 20 inch decks 3.0 to 4.0 GPM and up. Our <a href="/what-size-surface-cleaner-do-i-need/">surface cleaner sizing guide</a> and our explainer on <a href="/pressure-washer-psi-vs-gpm/">PSI vs GPM</a> cover why flow matters more than pressure here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rule out supply problems before blaming the washer. A kinked garden hose, a hose that is too long or narrow, a partly closed spigot or a clogged inlet screen all cut flow to the pump. Our guide to <a href="/pressure-washer-water-supply-requirements/">pressure washer water supply</a> lists what the pump needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the deck is simply too big for your washer, the honest fix is a smaller deck or nozzles sized for your flow. Our <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">surface cleaner rankings</a> note which attachments suit electric and lower-flow washers.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">QUICK TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Listen to the bar.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A steady, high-pitched whir means the bar is spinning well. A slow, pulsing or stuttering sound means it is starved for flow or binding. Fix that before adjusting technique.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="nozzles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cause 4: Nozzles, Bar Height and Deck Level
          </h2>

          <p>
            Worn or blocked nozzles unbalance the bar. A blocked nozzle stops one side from cleaning and makes the bar wobble; a worn nozzle sprays a wider, weaker fan. Either produces uneven arcs. Remove the nozzles and inspect them. Clear blockages with a nozzle cleaning pin, and replace worn nozzles as a matched pair or set.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check that the nozzles are the right size for your washer. Many decks ship with nozzles sized for a high-output gas machine; on a smaller washer, a larger orifice size can drop the pressure, while a much smaller one can over-pressurise. If the manufacturer publishes a nozzle chart, use it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bar height matters too. On adjustable decks, the distance between nozzles and the surface changes how concentrated the jets are. Too high and the jets spread and weaken; too low and the bar can hit high spots. Follow the manufacturer's setting and make sure all casters are the same height.
          </p>

          <p>
            A worn or missing caster tilts the deck, which brings one side's jets closer to the surface than the other's. That leaves a crescent on the same side of every pass. Spin each caster by hand and check that the deck sits level on a flat floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Swivel wear shows up in the same way as a flow problem. If the bar is hard to turn by hand or feels gritty, it will not reach full speed even with enough water. Many manufacturers sell swivel rebuild kits with new seals and bearings, which are usually cheaper than a new deck and restore the original spin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dirt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cause 5: Uneven Dirt, No Pre-Treat and No Rinse
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some streaks are not caused by the attachment at all. Concrete that is unevenly soiled, with mildew in shady areas and bare concrete in the sun, will not clean to an even colour in one pass. The surface cleaner removes a similar amount everywhere, so the dirtiest areas stay darker.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pre-treat organic growth, oil and rust before using the surface cleaner, and give the chemical its full dwell time. That evens out the starting point. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-use-detergent-in-a-pressure-washer/">using detergent in a pressure washer</a> covers application.
          </p>

          <p>
            The final rinse is the other common culprit. The surface cleaner lifts dirt into a slurry but does not move it off the slab. If that slurry dries in place, it leaves a haze, often with faint swirls that mirror the deck's path. Rinse the whole area downhill with a wide nozzle once you finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work downhill. Starting at the low end means dirty water runs back over the areas you just cleaned, redepositing dirt in streaks that follow the slope. Start at the high side and finish at the drain or street.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fix" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Fix a Slab That Already Has Streaks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most streaks can be blended out. Wait until the slab is dry so you can see exactly where they are, then re-clean the affected area with slow passes and generous overlap, running the lanes perpendicular to the original ones. Changing direction crosses the old bands and evens them out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat swirls by fixing the cause first. If the bar was spinning slowly, swap nozzles or use a smaller deck before re-cleaning; otherwise the second pass will leave the same pattern. If speed was the problem, halve your pace for the second pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse thoroughly afterwards and let the slab dry again before judging. Some faint lines on very old or stained concrete may never fully disappear, because the concrete itself has weathered unevenly. A light brightener or concrete cleaner can help even the tone on those slabs.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to seal, make sure the surface is even before you start, because sealer locks in whatever is there. Our guide to <a href="/pressure-wash-driveway-before-sealing/">pressure washing before sealing</a> covers the prep sequence and dry time.
          </p>

          <p>
            For slabs that keep streaking despite good technique, try a test patch in an out-of-the-way corner each time you change something. A two-by-two-foot square is enough to judge. It saves you re-cleaning the whole driveway three times while you work out whether the cause was speed, overlap or flow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Changing several things at once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you switch nozzles, slow down and change overlap all at the same time, you will never know which fixed it. Change one variable, test a small area, let it dry and look again. It takes longer but finds the cause.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the slab while it is wet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wet concrete hides streaks almost completely. A surface that looks perfect while wet can show clear bands an hour later. Always let the slab dry fully before deciding whether your technique worked or needs adjusting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming technique for a flow problem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the deck is too large for your washer's GPM, the bar spins too slowly to clean evenly however carefully you work. Check the washer's rating against the deck's recommended range before spending hours re-cleaning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring worn nozzles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nozzles wear with use, especially with gritty water. A worn nozzle sprays a wider, weaker fan and unbalances the bar. Inspect nozzles each season and replace them as a matched set when the spray pattern changes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the rinse to save time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The surface cleaner leaves dirt suspended in water on the slab. If it dries there, it leaves a haze or faint swirls. A quick wide-nozzle rinse downhill takes minutes and removes one of the most common causes of streaks.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my surface cleaner leave stripes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Straight stripes usually mean not enough overlap between passes. The outer edge of a spinning bar cleans less than the centre, so lanes placed side by side leave a light band at each edge. Overlap each pass by one-third to one-half of the deck width to blend them out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my surface cleaner leave swirl marks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Swirls or rainbow arcs usually mean the bar is not sweeping each spot enough times. Either you are moving too fast or the bar is spinning too slowly because the washer cannot supply enough flow. Slow down first, then check your washer's GPM against the deck's recommended range.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How fast should I move a surface cleaner to avoid streaks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A common guideline is about one foot every one to two seconds, slower on textured or very dirty surfaces. If you can see a distinct clean and dirty arc side by side immediately behind the deck, you are moving too fast for the bar to keep up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can the wrong nozzles cause surface cleaner streaks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Blocked or worn nozzles unbalance the bar and leave uneven arcs, and nozzles sized for a different washer can leave the bar under-powered. Check them with a nozzle cleaning pin, replace worn ones as a set, and use the manufacturer's nozzle chart for your washer's PSI and GPM.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a second pass remove surface cleaner streaks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually. Let the slab dry, fix the cause, then re-clean the affected area with slow, overlapping passes run perpendicular to the original lanes. Changing direction crosses the old bands. Rinse afterwards and let it dry again before judging the result.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do electric pressure washers cause more surface cleaner streaks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can, because electric washers typically deliver less flow than gas machines. A large deck designed for gas washers will spin slowly on an electric unit. A smaller deck or one built for electric washers spins at its intended speed and cleans more evenly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is there a haze after using my surface cleaner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A uniform haze is usually dried dirt slurry. The surface cleaner loosens dirt but leaves it suspended on the slab. Rinse the whole area downhill with a wide nozzle after cleaning, and work from the high side to the low side so dirty water never runs back over clean concrete.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

